---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'HTML to EPUB Troubleshooting: Missing Images & TOC Fixes | ChapterBind';
const description =
  'Diagnose and fix missing local images, broken internal links, duplicate IDs, layout differences, and reader compatibility issues when converting HTML to EPUB.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/html-to-epub-troubleshooting/">
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-8 sm:px-6 sm:py-12">
    <header class="rounded-2xl border border-black/10 bg-white p-6 shadow-xs sm:p-8">
      <div class="text-xs font-bold tracking-wider text-[#0a84ff] uppercase">Troubleshooting</div>
      <h1 class="display-heading mt-2 text-3xl font-extrabold text-[#1d1d1f] sm:text-4xl">
        HTML to EPUB Troubleshooting: Missing Images, TOC &amp; Reflow Fixes
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-relaxed text-[#515154]">
        Most conversion surprises come from relative file paths, remote image restrictions, or web-specific CSS that does not belong inside a reflowable ebook.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-5">
        <a
          href="/"
          class="btn-tactile inline-flex items-center rounded-xl bg-[#0a84ff] px-4 py-2 text-xs font-semibold text-white hover:bg-[#0070e0]"
        >
          Back to HTML to EPUB Converter →
        </a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-6">
      <section class="rounded-2xl border border-black/10 bg-white p-6 sm:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1d1d1f]">1. Missing Local Image Warnings (<code>MISSING_IMAGE</code>)</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[#515154]">
          If you upload only an <code>.html</code> file without its companion image folder, ChapterBind reports each unresolved <code>&lt;img src="..."&gt;</code> path as a warning instead of failing silently.
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm text-[#515154]">
          <li>
            <strong>Quick inline fix:</strong> Open the <strong>Diagnostics &amp; Issues</strong> panel and click <strong>Attach file</strong> beside any missing image path to map the image directly.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Re-import with folder:</strong> Click <strong>Select Folder</strong> and choose the parent directory containing both your HTML file and its <code>images/</code> or <code>_files/</code> subfolder.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Remote <code>https://</code> image URLs:</strong> ChapterBind never fetches external URLs during conversion. Save those images locally and update the relative path if you need them offline.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="rounded-2xl border border-black/10 bg-white p-6 sm:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1d1d1f]">2. Why Webpage Layout Changes in Reflowable EPUBs</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[#515154]">
          E-readers intentionally override webpage navigation bars, multi-column sidebars, and fixed pixel widths so text can wrap at any zoom level.
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm text-[#515154]">
          <li>ChapterBind strips raw <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> blocks, inline <code>style="..."</code> attributes, forms, iframes, and scripts, replacing them with a lightweight reflowable stylesheet (<code>EPUB/styles/ebook.css</code>).</li>
          <li>Images use <code>max-width: 100%; height: auto;</code> so wide diagrams shrink proportionally to fit narrow e-ink screens without clipping.</li>
          <li>If your HTML relies on interactive charts or JavaScript-rendered content, export static text and raster PNG/JPEG figures before converting.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="rounded-2xl border border-black/10 bg-white p-6 sm:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1d1d1f]">3. Missing or Out-of-Order Table of Contents Entries</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[#515154]">
          The EPUB 3 Table of Contents (<code>EPUB/nav.xhtml</code>) mirrors the chapter order in your workspace plus nested <code>h1</code>–<code>h3</code> headings inside each chapter.
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm text-[#515154]">
          <li>If a chapter lacks semantic heading tags (for instance, bold <code>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Title&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</code> instead of <code>&lt;h2&gt;</code>), only the top-level chapter title appears in the TOC.</li>
          <li>Rename chapters inline on the left panel before clicking <strong>Convert to EPUB</strong> to customize the TOC labels without editing your source HTML.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="rounded-2xl border border-black/10 bg-white p-6 sm:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1d1d1f]">4. Duplicate Image Filenames &amp; Broken Internal Links</h2>
        <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm text-[#515154]">
          <li>
            <strong>Duplicate image basenames across folders:</strong> If Chapter 1 uses <code>ch1/fig.png</code> and Chapter 2 uses <code>ch2/fig.png</code>, ChapterBind assigns distinct manifest names (<code>img-01-fig.png</code>, <code>img-02-fig.png</code>) so neither overwrites the other.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Unmatched local HTML links:</strong> Links pointing to HTML pages you did not import into the current book are converted to plain text and flagged under <code>BROKEN_INTERNAL_LINK</code> so EPUBCheck reports zero dead link errors.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="rounded-2xl border border-black/10 bg-white p-6 sm:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1d1d1f]">5. Reader Compatibility Notes</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[#515154]">
          Generated books conform to EPUB 3.0 / 3.3 and are verified with W3C EPUBCheck 5.1.0. Note that some older hardware e-readers with legacy EPUB 2 firmware may ignore advanced HTML5 landmarks while still displaying chapters, images, and the primary Table of Contents; testing a sample chapter on your target device is always recommended.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
